          "source_text": "The following remarks are obsolete as they are subsumed in\nHomology, Lemmas <REF_0001> and\n<REF_0002>.\nLet <MATH_0001> be an abelian category.\nLet <MATH_0002>\nbe a weak Serre subcategory (see\nHomology, Definition <REF_0003>).\nSuppose that <MATH_0003> is a double complex to which\nHomology, Lemma <REF_0004>\napplies such that for some <MATH_0004> all the objects\n<MATH_0005> belong to <MATH_0006>. Then all the cohomology groups\n<MATH_0007> belong to <MATH_0008>. Namely, the assumptions imply\nthat the kernels and images of <MATH_0009> are in <MATH_0010>.\nWhereupon we see that each <MATH_0011> is in <MATH_0012>.\nBy induction we see that each <MATH_0013> is in <MATH_0014>.\nHence each <MATH_0015> has a finite filtration whose subquotients\nare in <MATH_0016>. Using that <MATH_0017> is closed under extensions\nwe conclude that <MATH_0018> is in <MATH_0019> as claimed.\nThe same result holds for the second spectral sequence associated\nto <MATH_0020>. Similarly, if <MATH_0021> is a filtered\ncomplex to which\nHomology, Lemma <REF_0005>\napplies and for some <MATH_0022> all the objects <MATH_0023>\nbelong to <MATH_0024>, then each <MATH_0025> is\nan object of <MATH_0026>.\n",
          "source_text_hash": "sha256:242a7fc506743e43117d7e42c2fd788fc312db2f3a7d109aa74b3f2cca2e297c",
          "unit_id": "tag:076K:statement"
        }
